## 9. MAKE ONE-CLICK CHANGE SAFE

Inspect the existing Accept, Edit, Reject and correction-application handlers.

A suggested correction must not bypass the audit engine.

Implement this lifecycle:

1. Capture the original content and its version.
2. Associate the correction with the finding and relevant rule.
3. Validate the proposed replacement.
4. Check that it does not introduce prohibited terminology or contradict approved facts.
5. Ensure that the correction applies to the intended text occurrence.
6. Show the proposed change to the reviewer.
7. Apply it only after the required human action.
8. Create a new content version.
9. Preserve the original text and previous decisions.
10. Re-run applicable checks against the changed content.
11. Recalculate audit completeness and outstanding findings.
12. Require the appropriate approval before finalization.

Avoid global string replacements that accidentally modify unrelated occurrences.

Protect against stale edits. If the content has changed since the finding was generated, reject or reconcile the outdated patch rather than applying it blindly.

A correction must not automatically resolve an unrelated finding.

A draft must not be finalized simply because the user accepted one suggestion.

